# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: qt6-drawing-canvas-first-increment — a Qt6 interactive canvas on top of the cador engine (increment 1)
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature-proposal
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: qt6-canvas-first-increment
#+DATE: 2026-11-09

* Context — why this is a small increment, not a rewrite

cador is already a headless, AutoCAD-compatible 2D document engine (~42 k LOC):
a =DRAWING= object model with ~24 entity kinds, selection sets, a ~1900-line
system-variable catalogue, a vlax/ActiveX surface, geometry intersect, and
command modules (draw/edit/modify/hatch/array/geometry/file).  clautolisp has
the full AutoLISP/Visual LISP language, and DCL dialogs already render in a
real Qt process over an s-expression wire.

What is missing for anything that looks like AutoCAD is the *interactive
graphical canvas*: a viewport that draws the model, pans/zooms, and feeds
pointer/selection input back to =getpoint=/=entsel=/=ssget=.  This ticket
specifies the FIRST increment of exactly that, deliberately thin, reusing the
existing seams rather than inventing new ones.

NON-GOAL of this ticket: "a CAD application".  GOAL: the smallest canvas that
renders the cador model and makes interactive =getpoint=/=entsel=/=ssget= and
LISP =C:= commands work from the mouse, with pan/zoom — the foundation every
later increment builds on.

* The seams this reuses (measured, origin/master)

- *Transport:* the DCL Qt GUI is a subprocess over stdin/stdout speaking
  newline-framed s-expressions, grammar in lockstep in
  =clautolisp/autolisp-dcl/source/sexp-wire.lisp= (=*sexp-protocol-version* 1=)
  and =clautolisp/tools/clautolisp-gui-qt/source/sexp.{hpp,cpp}=.  The C++
  reader counts paren depth (pipe-safe) and dispatches one form per message on
  the main thread.  There is already a one-way raster channel —
  =(:image-paint DID KEY (PRIM...))= with =(:fill ...)= / =(:vector ...)=
  primitives painted by =DialogWindow::paintImage= via =QPainter=, with an
  ACI-index→QColor map (=aciToRgb=).  It is the closest existing analog to a
  canvas; it is strictly request/response and modal, with no upstream pointer
  events.
- *Model:* a drawing is a =DRAWING= CLOS value (=clautolisp/drawing/source/
  model.lisp=); entities are =entity-handle= structs whose =data= slot is the
  *DXF group-code alist* (=((0 . "LINE") (8 . "0") (10 x y z) (11 ...) ...)=),
  with =kind=, =block= (owner space =*Model_Space=/=*Paper_Space=/block), and
  =layer=.  The current space is enumerated by reversing
  =cador-creation-order= and filtering =main-space-entity-p= +
  =clautolisp.drawing:graphical-entity-p= — the exact loop =host-ssget "X"=
  uses (=selection-api.lisp=).
- *Input is injectable:* every =host-get*= reads one text line from
  =cador-prompt-stream= through the single chokepoint =read-prompt-line=
  (=cador/source/prompt-api.lisp=); that is how =--mock-input FILE= already
  drives the engine.  =host-getpoint= parses ="x,y[,z]"=; =host-entsel=
  hit-tests with =%pick-entity=.  A cooperative scheduler
  (=autolisp-runtime/source/scheduler.lisp=) already has a *park seam*,
  =%cador-maybe-park-read=, that yields instead of blocking when an
  interactive read would block.
- *Hit-testing already exists* in =cador/source/command-geometry.lisp=:
  =%pick-entity host point= (nearest within =*pick-tolerance*=) and
  =%window-selection host p1 p2 crossing= over =%entity-outline=.  Interactive
  =ssget= with =nil= mode currently signals =:host-unavailable-headless= — so
  the geometry is done; only the *wiring* is missing.

* Architecture decision (the one that matters)

*Out-of-process, reusing the proven sexp-over-stdio subprocess wire; the
canvas owns the view transform; Lisp owns hit-testing and the model.*

Rationale:
- Out-of-process keeps SBCL and Qt in separate address spaces (embedding Qt in
  the Lisp image is fragile); it reuses the existing, tested wire and its
  ACI→RGB map and paren-framed reader, and crashes stay isolated.
- The *canvas holds the scene in WORLD coordinates and does pan/zoom/extents
  locally*, so interaction is pure Qt with zero Lisp round-trip — the single
  most important UX/perf call.  Lisp re-sends the scene only on a model change
  (regen), as tessellated world-space polylines per entity.
- *Lisp keeps hit-testing* (=%pick-entity= / =%window-selection=): a click
  sends world coords up, Lisp resolves the entity/pickset.  No geometry logic
  is duplicated in C++.

This requires two protocol additions only (bump to =*sexp-protocol-version* 2=):
a non-modal canvas window, and upstream pointer/selection/command events.

* Protocol v2 additions (sexp-wire.lisp ↔ sexp.{hpp,cpp}/wire.cpp)

Downstream (runtime → canvas):
#+begin_example
(:open-canvas  CID NAME)
(:canvas-scene CID (ENT ...))     ; ENT = (HANDLE LAYER ACI (:poly x y x y ...) ...)
(:canvas-prompt CID TEXT)         ; echo the current prompt to the command line
(:canvas-mode  CID MODE)          ; :point | :pick | :window | :command | :idle
(:canvas-highlight CID (HANDLE...))
(:close-canvas CID STATUS)
#+end_example
Upstream (canvas → runtime):
#+begin_example
(:point   CID X Y)                ; left click in :point mode
(:pick    CID X Y)                ; left click in :pick mode -> Lisp %pick-entity
(:window  CID X1 Y1 X2 Y2 CROSSING)
(:key     CID CODE)
(:command CID STRING)             ; text entered in the canvas command line
#+end_example
Keep the existing =(:hello V)=, =(:error MSG)=, =(:done ...)=, =(:bye)=.  World
coordinates throughout; the canvas does the view transform, Lisp never sees
device pixels.

* Scope — IN (increment 1)

1. *Protocol v2* in =sexp-wire.lisp= + =sexp.{hpp,cpp}= (the messages above),
   with Lisp unit tests against a *mock wire* — no Qt needed — mirroring how
   DCL is tested.
2. *Scene exporter* (new, small, Lisp): walk the current space (the
   =host-ssget "X"= loop) and tessellate each =entity-handle= =data= alist to
   world-space polylines — LINE→segment, LWPOLYLINE/POLYLINE→segments,
   CIRCLE/ARC/ELLIPSE→polyline (reuse the geometry already in
   =command-geometry.lisp= / =intersect.lisp=), TEXT/MTEXT→baseline box
   placeholder, INSERT→block bounding box.  Tag each with handle + layer + ACI.
   Emit =(:canvas-scene ...)=.  Unit-tested on a fixture drawing.
3. *Qt6 canvas widget* (new C++, =QWidget= + =QPainter= first; =QOpenGLWidget=
   later): holds the world scene, draws polylines in ACI colours over a grid +
   axes + crosshair; pan (middle-drag), zoom (wheel, cursor-anchored),
   zoom-extents (key); a command-line =QLineEdit= at the bottom.  Reuses
   =aciToRgb=.  Non-modal (new run mode beside the modal DCL dialog loop).
4. *Input bridge* (Lisp): while a =get*=/=entsel=/=ssget= is pending, put the
   canvas in the matching =:canvas-mode=; convert the upstream
   =(:point|:pick|:window ...)= to the text/ename the prompt path expects and
   feed =cador-prompt-stream= (or resume the parked read via
   =%cador-maybe-park-read=).  This makes bare =(getpoint)= / =(entsel)= /
   =(ssget)= and any LISP =C:= command work from the mouse *unchanged*.
5. *Command line + regen loop*: =(:command CID STRING)= is evaluated on the
   same path as =repl-loop= (so =(command "LINE" "0,0" "10,10" "")= and LISP
   =C:= commands both run); after each top-level eval that changes =dbmod=, the
   scene is re-exported and re-sent.  Coarse "regen after every form" first;
   incremental later.
6. A =--gui-canvas= CLI flag (beside =--gui=) selecting this mode; env
   =CLAUTOLISP_GUI_CANVAS=.

* Scope — OUT (named, so the boundary is honest)

- *Interactive engine draw commands pulling live points.*  =host-command='s
  =%cmd-*= handlers consume TOKEN arguments and never read the prompt-stream
  (documented FUTURE park point D1 §13.5).  So in increment 1 the built-in
  =LINE= command still takes tokens; live "click-to-draw with the built-in
  command" is *increment 2* (wire =%cmd-*= to park on canvas input).  Increment
  1 delivers interactive input through =get*=/=entsel=/=ssget= + LISP commands,
  which is enough to prove the loop end to end.
- Rubber-band / drag preview of an in-progress command; grips; osnap; object
  snap tracking; dynamic input.  (A crosshair yes; a rubber-band to the pending
  point needs command-level feedback — increment 2+.)
- 3D: ortho top view only, Z ignored.  No visual styles, no OpenGL shading.
- Fidelity: no true text glyphs (box placeholder), no hatch fill (boundary
  only), no linetypes/lineweights (solid 1 px), no dimension rendering.
- No DWG open/save from the canvas UI (load via existing CLI / AutoLISP).
- No Qt app shell (ribbon, properties/layer palettes, layout tabs) — a single
  canvas window + command line only.
- =nentsel= (nested selection) — not implemented in cador yet; out of scope.

* Buildable slices (each independently testable)

- *A. Protocol v2* (sexp-wire.lisp + sexp.{hpp,cpp}) + Lisp mock-wire tests.  No Qt.
- *B. Scene exporter* (entity-handle → world polylines) + fixture unit tests.
- *C. Qt6 canvas widget*: render scene, pan/zoom/extents, crosshair, command line.
- *D. Input bridge*: point/pick/window → prompt-stream / scheduler resume, so
  =getpoint=/=entsel=/=ssget= work from the mouse.
- *E. Command line + regen-on-change*: the end-to-end LISP-command demo.

* Acceptance criteria

- =clautolisp --gui-canvas= opens a Qt6 window: empty modelspace, grid, axes,
  crosshair; pan (middle-drag), wheel-zoom (cursor-anchored) and zoom-extents
  all work *locally* with no Lisp round-trip.
- =(command "LINE" "0,0" "10,10" "")= (REPL or canvas command line) draws a
  line that appears after regen.
- =(getpoint)= puts the canvas in point mode; a click returns the world point
  as the call's result and the command line echoes the prompt.
- =(entsel)= → clicking a rendered line returns its ename (via =%pick-entity=)
  and highlights it.
- =(ssget)= window/crossing drag returns a pickset of the enclosed/crossed
  entities (via =%window-selection=).
- A sample LISP =C:= command that calls =getpoint= + =entsel= runs end to end
  from the mouse.
- A fixture drawing with LINE/CIRCLE/ARC/LWPOLYLINE/TEXT(box)/INSERT(bbox)
  renders correctly in ortho top view.
- CI: slices A, B, D are covered by Lisp unit tests against a mock wire (no Qt);
  the Qt build/run is a manual/gui lane, as the DCL Qt tool already is.

* Rough effort (agent-fleet framing)

Small by the standards of this project — the headless half exists.  Order of a
*few agent-weeks* of fleet work plus human steering on the canvas *feel* (the
part that does not compress): pan/zoom cursor-anchoring, crosshair, pick
tolerance in device vs world units, regen cadence.  Slices A/B/D are mostly
mechanical and testable; slice C is where human visual judgement is the limiter.

This increment is the enabling foundation.  Increment 2 (wire =%cmd-*= to park
on live canvas input → click-to-draw with the built-in commands; rubber-band;
osnap/grips) and increment 3 (OpenGL, 3D, fidelity: glyphs/hatch/linetypes,
the Qt app shell) are separate tickets.

* Note

Written as a design sketch at pjb's request while estimating the gap between
clautolisp and a full AutoCAD clone.  It commits no code; it fixes the
architecture decision (out-of-process, world-space canvas, Lisp-side
hit-testing) and the minimal protocol so a first increment is a few testable
slices rather than an open-ended rewrite.

* Alternative target — near AutoCAD/BricsCAD parity, built 100% by AI agents

The increment above is step one toward a larger north star, specified here so
the first increment is understood as serving it.

** The target

Behave, for a user, close to AutoCAD/BricsCAD.  Because AutoCAD and BricsCAD
themselves differ in many behaviours, "close to one or the other" gives
latitude: choose ONE coherent behaviour per feature instead of cloning a
specific product bug-for-bug.  Backend Common Lisp (clautolisp + cador), UI
Qt6, on Windows / macOS / Linux.

** Auxiliary goals — and why they are also the ENABLERS of an agent build

Each goal is not just a requirement; it is what makes a 100%-agent build
*verifiable*, and therefore fast:

- *Every command has a =-command= headless form* (the SCR script + AutoLISP +
  cador command-token path already lean this way) => every feature is
  driveable without the mouse, so agents write deterministic command-sequence
  tests, not brittle UI-click tests.
- *GUI detachable from the windowing system* (Qt =-platform offscreen= /
  =QOffscreenSurface=; the increment-1 world-space canvas already renders to a
  surface that can be a =QImage=) => *screenshot-diff regression tests* with no
  display server, in CI, on every MR.  This is the single biggest multiplier
  for agent-built GUI work: it turns "does it look right" into a diffable
  artefact an agent iterates against unattended.
- *Scriptable/testable throughout* => the correctness oracle is "same result as
  the recorded reference / same pixels as the approved baseline", checkable by
  an agent without a human in the loop.

In short, the auxiliary goals convert subjective CAD/GUI work into
deterministic, headless, diffable tests — the precondition under which an agent
fleet is fast and safe.  A conventional click-only GUI would NOT be
agent-friendly; this architecture is chosen so that it is.

** Architecture (reuse — do not write — the hard kernels)

- *Backend:* clautolisp + cador (document model, ~24 entity kinds, sysvars,
  selection, vlax, commands) — already ~42 k LOC and headless.
- *2D render + interaction:* the Qt6 canvas of increment 1, grown to the full
  2D pipeline (lineweights, linetypes, SHX + TrueType glyphs, hatch fill,
  transparency, draw order, annotation scaling), grips, all osnap modes +
  object/polar tracking, dynamic input, the command line, palettes, ribbon or
  classic UI, model/layout tabs.
- *3D kernel:* INTEGRATE OpenCASCADE (do not generate a kernel): solids,
  surfaces, booleans, tessellation; 3D visual styles via OpenGL; orbit/viewcube.
- *DWG/DXF:* libredwg (read) + the R2018-write fork, extended toward full
  object fidelity; the ACIS solid stream + dynamic-block + dimension long tail
  is the hard part, validated by round-trip against real AutoCAD/BricsCAD.
- *Offscreen:* the renderer targets EITHER an on-screen =QOpenGLWidget= OR an
  offscreen surface / =QImage=, selected at runtime, so screenshots and CI run
  headless.

** Milestones (calendar, agent fleet + small human steering team)

- *M1 — interactive 2D canvas* (this ticket): render model, pan/zoom,
  getpoint/entsel/ssget + LISP commands from the mouse.  ~1-2 months.
- *M2 — usable 2D CAD* (behaves like BricsCAD in 2D): click-to-draw built-in
  commands (wire =%cmd-*= to park on canvas input), grips, osnap/tracking,
  dynamic input, the full 2D draw/modify set, layer + properties palettes, DWG
  2D round-trip, plot-to-PDF, offscreen screenshot tests in CI.  ~6-12 months.
- *M3 — fidelity + annotation + blocks:* full associative dimensioning, tables,
  fields, dynamic blocks + block editor, geometric/dimensional constraints +
  solver, hatch fidelity, xrefs, layouts / viewports / plot styles.  +~1-1.5 yr.
- *M4 — 3D:* OpenCASCADE integration, solids/surfaces, 3D visual styles,
  navigation, 3D DWG/ACIS round-trip.  +~1-2 years (the kernel sets the pace).
- *M5 — parity polish:* CUIX / menus / DIESEL, the long tail of dialogs,
  large-file performance (spatial index, incremental regen), cross-platform
  packaging / signing / notarisation, compatibility hardening.  +~1-1.5 yr,
  overlapping M3/M4.

** Effort vs time — the honest numbers

- *Raw scope* (human-equivalent) for near-parity: ~*150-400 person-years*.
  Calibration: BricsCAD took Bricsys ~15-20 years and a team that grew into the
  hundreds, AND they licensed a kernel (ACIS) and ODA for DWG rather than
  writing them.  Reusing OpenCASCADE + libredwg + Qt + the existing cador engine
  removes a large fraction but not the long tail.
- *With a 100% AI-agent build* + a small human steering/validation team (~2-5
  people): near-parity calendar ≈ *3-6 years*, with a genuinely useful
  BricsCAD-like *2D CAD in ~1 year* (M2).  The agent leverage appears as
  calendar compression (3-6 yr vs BricsCAD's 15-20), not as making the work
  small: ~60-80 % of the CODE can be agent-authored, and the clautolisp/cador
  history (~157 k LOC built largely by agent sessions in months) is direct
  evidence.  "100 % AI-authored" does NOT mean "no human time".

** What does NOT compress (this sets the floor, not code volume)

- *The geometry kernel* — integrate and tame OpenCASCADE; boolean/tolerance
  edge cases are slow human-judgement debugging.
- *The UX feel loop* — snap/drag/dynamic-input ergonomics; screenshot-diff tests
  compress it a lot, but a human still sets the bar each round.
- *DWG write fidelity for hard objects* (ACIS streams, dynamic blocks) — gated
  by binary round-trip validation against real AutoCAD/BricsCAD.
- *Performance at scale* — profiling on real 100 MB+ drawings.
- *Human review + validation bandwidth* — the hard cap; CAD correctness bugs are
  subtle, and (pjb's own point) human intervention both steers and rate-limits.

** Deliberate scope cuts (the AutoCAD/BricsCAD-divergence margin buys these)

- No binary ObjectARX / .NET ABI compatibility (cannot be cloned); the
  extension API is AutoLISP + a documented Lisp/Qt API.  Third-party ARX/.NET
  plug-ins will not load — accepted.
- Where AutoCAD and BricsCAD differ, pick one coherent behaviour rather than
  maintaining a per-product compatibility matrix.
- Niche subsystems (point clouds, BIM, parametric sheet-metal, photorealistic
  render) are OUT of "near parity" unless separately scoped.

* Alternative 2 — embed clautolisp via ECL, with a C/C++ extension API (CRX)

** The idea

Port clautolisp (already ANSI CL on SBCL + CCL) to *ECL* (Embeddable Common
Lisp), compile it as a library, and *link it into the Qt6 application* — ONE
process, the Lisp engine + cador document + Qt UI sharing one address space.
This is the AutoCAD/BricsCAD architecture (embedded engine + native SDK), and it
unlocks a C/C++ extension API, *"CRX"* (the ObjectARX/BRX analog): third-party
shared libraries (=.dll= / =.dylib= / =.so=) loaded into the app that register
commands, define custom entities/objects, hook reactors and draw, through a
stable C ABI.  ECL is the enabler — it is callable FROM C and calls INTO C, so
the two-way bridge is clean.

** What it buys over the out-of-process design (Alternative 1)

- *In-process:* the canvas reads the cador model directly (no scene serialised
  over a pipe), reactors update the UI live, modeless palettes bind to live Lisp
  objects, far lower latency.  Closer to AutoCAD/BricsCAD.
- *A real C/C++ SDK (CRX):* native-code extensions, not only AutoLISP — the
  thing ARX/BRX provide and that a serious CAD ecosystem needs.
- No cross-process protocol to design/maintain (removes the sexp-wire tax for
  the production path).

** What it costs (new work vs Alternative 1)

- *ECL port* of ~157 k LOC: already portable (runs on SBCL + CCL, so most
  impl-specific code is abstracted), but ECL has real quirks — compiled code
  slower than SBCL (keep hot geometry/regen in C++/OpenCASCADE, leave
  commands/AutoLISP in ECL), its FFI, its thread model, unicode/external-format
  setup, and mapping the cooperative scheduler (thread-parked continuations)
  onto Qt's event loop.  ~2-6 months, agent-heavy but gated by ECL-specific
  debugging; adds a 3rd CI lane.
- *Embedding* into the Qt app: GC/thread discipline (which thread runs Lisp),
  signal handling, ECL↔Qt call bridges, lifecycle, the scheduler/event-loop
  marriage.  Fiddly, judgement-heavy integration.  ~2-4 months.
- *CRX C/C++ SDK* (the big new deliverable): the versioned C header SDK (entity
  access, command registration, selection, prompts, transactions, reactors,
  custom-object derivation, draw/snap/grip protocols), the platform loader
  (=dlopen= / =LoadLibrary=), the marshalling bridge C-ABI ↔ cador Lisp objects,
  ABI-stability discipline, samples + docs.  Scope-dependent: a USABLE CRX
  (commands, entity CRUD, selection, prompts, reactors, simple custom objects)
  ~6-12 months; a BROAD CRX (full custom-entity graphics/snap/grip protocols,
  matching more of ARX) +1-2 years.

** Trade-offs

- *Crash isolation lost:* in-process, a bad CRX module or an ECL/Qt fault takes
  the whole app down — exactly the ARX trade AutoCAD made.  (Optional mitigation:
  run untrusted CRX in a sandbox/subprocess.)
- *Performance:* ECL < SBCL; the mitigation (heavy geometry in C++/OCCT, ECL for
  orchestration) is a natural split but a real architectural constraint.
- *ABI is a long-term commitment:* a stable C ABI to maintain across releases;
  design it right once.

** Recommended sequencing

Keep increment 1's out-of-process canvas to validate the render/interaction
model cheaply, but treat the *ECL embed as the production architecture* from M2
on: the world-space canvas + command-token design port to in-process cleanly,
and CRX is only possible in-process.

** Estimate — 100 % agent vs hybrid team

- *Scope:* Alternative 1's ~150-400 person-years PLUS the ECL port + embedding +
  CRX SDK (~another 1-2 years of calendar work, overlapping).
- *100 % AI-agent build* + small steering team: ~*4-7 years* to
  near-parity-WITH-a-C-extension-API; a usable 2D CAD still ~1-1.5 years (the
  ECL port + embed land early so M2 is already in-process).
- *Hybrid team* (expert humans on the gnarly bits — ECL embedding, CRX ABI
  design, kernel integration, perf; agents on the breadth): ~*3-5 years*, and
  crucially LOWER RISK.  For THIS option the hybrid advantage is LARGER than for
  Alternatives 1-2, because its hard parts (embedding threading/GC, a stable C
  ABI) are *expertise-bound, not breadth-bound* — exactly where a human expert
  beats agent trial-and-error, which on ABI/embedding work is slow and
  dangerous.  A Lisp/ECL expert nails the embed in weeks; a CAD-SDK architect
  designs CRX right the first time; agents then fill the enormous breadth behind
  them.

** Why this is the right destination anyway

Even though it is slightly more work than Alternative 1, *in-process-embedded +
C/C++ SDK IS the AutoCAD/BricsCAD architecture*.  If the goal is genuine parity
including a native extension ecosystem, this is the target; Alternative 1's
out-of-process wire is a bootstrap, not the destination.

* Estimate basis — recalibrated from agent throughput, not human teams

*Honest correction (pjb's challenge, 2026-11-09).*  The year-scale figures in
the sections above were anchored on HUMAN-team calibration (BricsCAD ~15-20
years, hundreds of engineers) with a compression factor applied — not computed
bottom-up from observed agent throughput.  That is the wrong basis, and it
over-estimates.  Recomputing:

- *Observed throughput on THIS codebase:* cador + the engine (~157 k LOC of
  tested, reviewed CAD-domain Lisp) were built largely by agent sessions over
  *months* — on the order of 10-30 k LOC/month of MERGED, tested code — and
  individual well-specified features that a human plan would call "weeks" land
  in a day or two.  That is the number to extrapolate from, not a human-year.
- *Code volume is NOT the gate.*  With a fleet (and more compute scales it
  further), the throughput-bound work — GUI breadth, 2D fidelity, the command
  set, DWG mapping, CRX breadth — collapses toward its validation floor.  The
  per-option "person-years" above are a *human-equivalent SCOPE* measure (an
  upper bound), not a calendar.

** What actually sets the calendar (latency floors, independent of agent count)

1. *Real-CAD validation round-trips* — DWG/behaviour parity is checked against a
   real AutoCAD/BricsCAD, i.e. the intermittent Windows/CAD runner; those jobs
   already wait hours-to-days for a slot.  Dedicated always-on CAD validation
   hardware moves this floor more than any coding speed does.
2. *3D geometry-kernel robustness* — integrating OpenCASCADE is fast; taming
   boolean/tolerance failures on REAL models is an empirical, long-tailed
   debugging loop.  This is the long pole; agents help but do not remove it.
3. *Human review / decision bandwidth* — architecture calls, "does it behave
   right" judgements, merge gating (pjb's own caveat).  If the human is the
   bottleneck, calendar = human attention, not agent speed.
4. *UX feel approval* — compressed hugely by the offscreen screenshot-diff tests
   (an auxiliary goal), but a human still sets the bar each round.

** Agent-computed calendar (supersedes the year figures above)

Assumes a fleet + enough validation hardware (always-on CAD runners) + a
responsive reviewer:

| milestone                                   | agent-computed calendar      |
|---------------------------------------------+------------------------------|
| M1 interactive 2D canvas (this ticket)      | days - ~2 weeks              |
| M2 usable 2D CAD (BricsCAD-like 2D)         | ~2 weeks - 3 months          |
| M3 fidelity: dims / tables / blocks / constr| ~1 - 4 months                |
| M4 3D via OpenCASCADE + ACIS round-trip     | ~3 - 12 months  (long pole)  |
| M5 polish / perf / packaging (overlaps)     | ~1 - 3 months                |
| *Near-parity total (Alt 1 / 2)*             | *~6 - 18 months*             |
| Alt 3 add: ECL port + embed + usable CRX    | +~2 - 8 months               |
| *Near-parity + native CRX (Alt 3)*          | *~9 - 24 months*             |

The spread is dominated by the 3D-kernel / real-CAD-validation tail (floors 1-2),
NOT by how much code there is.  Resource the real floors (always-on CAD
validation, generous compute, a dedicated reviewer) and these land near the LOW
end; starve them and near the high end.  Honest residual uncertainty: no full
CAD has been built this way, so M4 especially is a projection — but it is now
computed from agent throughput + real latency floors, not from a human-team
analogy.  The person-year numbers in the earlier sections should be read as
"human-equivalent scope", i.e. the upper bound, and this table as the expected
calendar.

* 3D module — separable, demand-gated

3D (OpenCASCADE kernel + solid-modelling UI + ACIS round-trip) is designed as a
SEPARATE module behind a clean boundary, built later or sooner per customer
demand; the 2D product (M1-M3) ships without it.  The split is clean because
*DWG 3D interop is kernel-level, not UI-level*: 3D solids are ACIS streams in
the DWG whatever UI drew them, so file compatibility is the ACIS/OCCT
round-trip, decoupled from the 3D UX.  And AutoCAD vs BricsCAD *diverge* in 3D
UX, which is latitude, not a constraint — pick ONE coherent UX rather than
cloning either.  Recommendation when 3D is scoped: direct-modelling COMMANDS (a
la BricsCAD =DM*=, the most scriptable/testable, fitting the =-command= goal) +
a manipulator + an optional quad-like context HUD.

Training-knowledge summary of the divergence (UNVERIFIED — see the task below):
- *AutoCAD:* ribbon 3D workspaces, subobject gizmos, =PRESSPULL=, ViewCube;
  direct (NON-parametric) 3D; ShapeManager (an ACIS fork).
- *BricsCAD:* the Quad (context HUD at the cursor), the Manipulator, the =DM*=
  direct-modelling command family, AND 3D geometric/dimensional constraints +
  design-intent recognition (parametric 3D, which AutoCAD lacks); LookFrom;
  BIM / Sheet-Metal / Mechanical verticals on the same ACIS core.
- Both rest on ACIS, so solids interoperate at the file level.

